034bc188b5bc133ebd31a8c658eb87dca5a73e530cd5ce","clauses":[],"mapped_clause_count":null,"status":"unknown","whole_claim_coverage":"unknown"},"classification":{"evidence_class":"semantic_validator","family":"import_projection_and_drift","real_substrate_disposition":"real_substrate_capsule","truth_accounting_bucket":"real_import_validation"},"component_id":"tool_server_pressure_inventory","evidence_state":{"current_backend_binding":{"status":"not_checked"},"fresh_prover":{"status":"not_recorded_by_index"},"fresh_replay":{"status":"not_recorded_by_index"},"index_state":"retained_generated_snapshot"},"guide":{"availability":"available","guide_id":"paper_module.tool_server_pressure_inventory","plain":"When too many programs are running on a computer, someone has to work out which ones belong to whom. This check is given a saved snapshot of the running programs, a list classifying their owners and a policy on how much pressure is too much. It follows ownership links, sorts owners into active, unknown and detached, applies the policy, and rejects any claim that a program with an unknown owner is safe to close. The output is an inventory and a recommendation for easing the pressure, to help decide what needs an owner's attention.","plain_availability":"available","ref":"paper_modules/tool_server_pressure_inventory.md","sha256":"fb6bdd0659f297509c11045e80feacb5238dad997cf76a01e82fc12858dfa8f3","text":"# Classify tool-server process pressure\n\n> **In plain terms.** When too many programs are running on a computer, someone has to work out which ones belong to whom. This check is given a saved snapshot of the running programs, a list classifying their owners and a policy on how much pressure is too much. It follows ownership links, sorts owners into active, unknown and detached, applies the policy, and rejects any claim that a program with an unknown owner is safe to close. The output is an inventory and a recommendation for easing the pressure, to help decide what needs an owner's attention.\n\nThis component takes a supplied process table, owner classifications and pressure policy and produces an inventory and a relief recommendation. It helps inspect a recorded process snapshot before deciding what needs an owner’s attention.\n\n## Run the example\n\nFrom the repository root, with Python 3.11 or newer available:\n\n```bash\nPYTHONPATH=src python3 -m microcosm_core.organs.tool_server_pressure_inventory run \\\n  --input fixtures/first_wave/tool_server_pressure_inventory/input \\\n  --out .microcosm/component-guides/tool_server_pressure_inventory \\\n  --acceptance-out .microcosm/component-guides/tool_server_pressure_inventory/acceptance.json\n```\n\nThe command writes local output under `.microcosm/component-guides/`. Check the returned status and the result receipt; a blocked result reports findings for the supplied inputs.\n\n## What this checks\n\nThe classifier follows owner relationships, distinguishes active, unknown and detached owners, and applies the supplied policy. Tests cover cycles and active-owner descendants. It rejects a claim that an unknown-owner process is safe to close, and rejects recorded process signals or exposed command previews.\n\n## Limits\n\nClassifies the supplied tool-server process list by owner and policy and recommends pressure relief, rejecting a claim that an unknown-owner process is safe to close and any recorded process signal.\n\n## Verification\n\nTests exercise specific cases; no component-specific formal proof of its algorithm is established. When its exported-bundle route runs through `make component-replays`, the [shared Dafny-verified admission check](../formal/replay/README.md) checks agreement of source, input, claim-policy and receipt observations. The direct fixture command above does not itself run that check.\n\n## Source\n\n[Implementation](../src/microcosm_core/organs/tool_server_pressure_inventory.py) · [Example input](../fixtures/first_wave/tool_server_pressure_inventory/input/) · 